= Persuasive Writing: Two-Session Lesson Plan

*Grade 8 | Two 50-minute sessions | Unit: Argument and Persuasion*

== Learning Objectives

By the end of this lesson, students will be able to:

- Identify the three classical appeals (ethos, pathos, logos) in a persuasive text.
- Write a clear claim and support it with at least two pieces of relevant evidence.
- Acknowledge and respond to a counterclaim.
- Revise a persuasive paragraph based on peer feedback.

== Materials

- Projector or board for displaying model texts
- Printed copies of two short persuasive passages (one advertisement, one opinion letter to the editor)
- Sticky notes in three colors, one color for each appeal
- Graphic organizer: Claim, Evidence 1, Evidence 2, Counterclaim, Response
- Peer review checklist
- Exit ticket slips

== Session 1 (50 minutes)

=== Warm-up: Would You Rather? (8 minutes)

Pose a question such as "Should schools start after 9:00 a.m.?" Ask students to stand on one side of the room or the other. Have two volunteers from each side give one reason for their position. Point out that every student just tried to persuade someone, even without knowing it.

=== Mini-Lesson: The Three Appeals (12 minutes)

Introduce the three appeals with a quick definition and one example each.

#table(
  columns: 3,
  [*Appeal*], [*Definition*], [*Example*],
  [Ethos], [Credibility or trustworthiness of the writer], [A pediatrician explains why sleep matters for teens.],
  [Pathos], [Emotional connection with the audience], [A story about a student who fell asleep during a test.],
  [Logos], [Logic, facts, and reasoning], [Survey data showing teens who sleep more earn higher grades.],
)

Display the advertisement passage and ask students to name the appeals they notice. Model how to underline or annotate one example of each.

=== Guided Practice: Sticky Note Hunt (15 minutes)

- Put students in pairs and give each pair the opinion letter.
- Assign each pair a color: yellow for ethos, pink for pathos, blue for logos.
- Pairs read the letter and place one sticky note on each example they find.
- Each pair shares one note with the class and explains why it fits its category.

Listen for confusion between pathos and logos. A useful test: does the sentence make the reader *feel* something (pathos) or *think* something through (logos)?

=== Independent Practice: Choose Your Stance (12 minutes)

Students choose one prompt and write a single claim sentence on the graphic organizer.

+ Schools should allow students to use phones during lunch.
+ Cities should build more bike lanes.
+ Students should be required to volunteer in the community each year.
+ Your own topic, approved by the teacher.

Then have students list two possible pieces of evidence and label each with the appeal it uses.

=== Exit Ticket (3 minutes)

Students answer on a slip of paper:

+ Write your claim in one sentence.
+ Name one appeal you plan to use and explain why.

